  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 215 AM CDT Fri Aug 28 2026\n\nToday and Tomorrow... Another morning with clear skies and \nample cooling. This will allow for more fog formation again this\nmorning, which will once again lift quickly after sunrise as \ntemperatures warm. There could be some isolated dense fog in \nlow lying areas and near bodies of water, but overall widespread\ndense fog is not expected. Overall a very similar setup to \nyesterday morning, so expecting a similar result. The high \npressure that has given us this stretch of weather will be \nmoving out today. Our next system will begin to move in from \nwest to east. During the day this will be little more than \ngradually increasing clouds. A subtle mid level shortwave \ncombined with the advection of warmer and more moist air will \nprovide the chance for rain tonight into tomorrow. Forecast \nsoundings continue to show the chance for some isolated \nthunderstorms within this rain, but the chances for strong to \nsevere storms remain low. The amount of instability and lapse \nrates are just not supportive of severe convection and SPC has \nalso maintained a general thunder with the marginal risk off\nover the Dakotas. Today will be slightly warmer with highs in \nthe 80s owing the the WAA. Tomorrow will be a little cooler, \nhighs in the upper 70s to lower 80s, with the rain moving \nthrough providing for increased cloud cover.\n\nSunday into next week... This still looks to be a progressive\npattern with multiple waves moving into the Upper Midwest. As\nsaid in past discussions there remains a lot of spread within\nguidance and especially ensemble members. The only part of the\nforecast with clustering is the Friday night into Saturday rain,\nafter that it remains very dispersive. What is more confident\nare warmer than normal temperatures. Sunday into Monday look to\nsee highs well above normal in the mid to upper 80s.",
